n777\t              \"practical_G90\",\n778\t              1240,\n779\t              3,\n780\t              4,\n781\t              0.901,\n782\t              \"[0.887, 0.918]\"\n783\t            ],\n784\t            [\n785\t              \"full_mmlu\",\n786\t              14042,\n787\t              6,\n788\t              6,\n789\t              0.972,\n790\t              \"[0.968, 0.979]\"\n791\t            ]\n792\t          ]\n793\t        }\n794\t      },\n795\t      {\n796\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Fixed vs random facet sensitivity (MD2)\",\n797\t        \"data\": {\n798\t          \"columns\": [\n799\t            \"configuration\",\n800\t            \"G_binary\",\n801\t            \"delta_from_all_random\"\n802\t          ],\n803\t          \"rows\": [\n804\t            [\n805\t              \"all_random\",\n806\t              0.594,\n807\t              0.0\n808\t            ],\n809\t            [\n810\t              \"temp_fixed\",\n811\t              0.628,\n812\t              0.034\n813\t            ],\n814\t            [\n815\t              \"ordering_fixed\",\n816\t              0.641,\n817\t              0.047\n818\t            ],\n819\t            [\n820\t              \"temp+ordering_fixed\",\n821\t              0.664,\n822\t              0.07\n823\t            ]\n824\t          ]\n825\t        }\n826\t      },\n827\t      {\n828\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Bootstrap vs Jackknife CI comparison (MD6)\",\n829\t        \"data\": {\n830\t          \"columns\": [\n831\t            \"component\",\n832\t            \"bootstrap_ci_width\",\n833\t            \"jackknife_ci_width\",\n834\t            \"ratio\"\n835\t          ],\n836\t          \"rows\": [\n837\t            [\n838\t              \"item_id\",\n839\t              8.6,\n840\t              8.4,\n841\t              1.02\n842\t            ],\n843\t            [\n844\t              \"residual\",\n845\t              7.8,\n846\t              8.1,\n847\t              0.96\n848\t            ],\n849\t            [\n850\t              \"model_x_item\",\n851\t              9.7,\n852\t              9.4,\n853\t              1.03\n854\t            ],\n855\t            [\n856\t              \"prompt_x_item\",\n857\t              3.1,\n858\t              3.0,\n859\t              1.03\n860\t            ],\n861\t            [\n862\t              \"G_binary\",\n863\t              0.081,\n864\t              0.079,\n865\t              1.03\n866\t            ]\n867\t          ]\n868\t        }\n869\t      }\n870\t    ],\n871\t    \"figures\": [],\n872\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-004在worst档中的核心价值：IRT对比(3.3×)和方法学robustness package。 IRT对比是self-contained的——不需要4-model结果或cross-benchmark数据，完全基于 已完成的单模型Llama MMLU数据。3.3×的ratio比原预期低，但仍是\\\"首个G-theory vs IRT直接对比\\\"的文献贡献。Bootstrap CIs的宽度(model×item CI包含接近0的值)进一步 支持将model×item降级为\\\"preliminary\\\"的策略。方法学robustness的6项验证全部 基于exp-001 Llama数据，不受worst档的数据缺失影响——这是论文的\\\"guaranteed floor\\\"。 Missing analyses: hardware crossing(exp-003缺失)、full cross-benchmark gradient (exp-002不完整)、full logprob analysis across benchmarks(只能做MMLU)。 论文需要在Methods中明确标注哪些分析是\\\"planned but not completed\\\"。\\n\\n\\nIRT vs G-theory对比是worst档中最重要的surviving finding。ratio降至3.3-3.5× (vs原预期的3.6-4.4×)因为：(1) IRT在4-model数据上估计更保守(142 items vs 原2-model数据的116 items)，缩小了分母差距；(2) G-theory的D-study在4-model 分析中因model×item降至7.3%而需要fewer items来compensate，numerator从510 降至498。但3.3×仍然是substantial——意味着IRT忽略的facets(prompt, seed, ordering) 贡献了约70%的额外item需求。这个finding不依赖model×item的magnitude，因此 在worst档中仍然robust。 Bootstrap CIs显示item_id的95% CI为[56.8, 65.4]，range=8.6pp——与exp-001b的 resampling变异一致，进一步确认point estimate的精度有限。model×item的4-model CI为[3.1, 12.8]，range很宽(9.7pp)，包含0附近——reviewer可能argue \\\"model×item is not reliably different from zero with only 4 models\\\"。这是对原Claim 1的 又一打击：不仅point estimate从26%降至7%，CI还包含了trivially small values。 Fixed vs random sensitivity分析显示delta仅0.070——这是一个正面结果，说明 facet classification choice对G影响不大，MD2 defense成立。 D-study投影到14K items给出G=0.972——这回应了MD5 \\\"MMLU is unreliable\\\"的误读， 但468 items for G≥0.80(vs原文510)的变化需要更新所有D-study tables。\\n\"\n873\t  },\n874\t  {\n875\t    \"name\": \"exp-005: 14B model extension\",\n876\t    \"description\": \"Qwen3-14B和DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B在datacenter GPU上的BF16/FP16测试。 在worst档中，因exp-003缺失(deps)且无可用的48GB+ VRAM GPU而完全未执行。\\n\",\n877\t    \"results\": {\n878\t      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n879\t      \"blocking_reason\": \"Depends on exp-003 (not completed); no 48GB+ VRAM GPU available\",\n880\t      \"records_collected\": 0\n881\t    },\n882\t    \"tables\": [],\n883\t    \"figures\": [],\n884\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-005缺失意味着reviewer concern #6 \\\"Missing scale validation\\\"只能在Limitations 中acknowledge。论文需要argue \\\"7-9B models represent the most commonly used open-weight model class\\\"并cite usage statistics。但sophisticated reviewer会 指出14B+模型可能有qualitatively different variance structure(更稳定的internal representations → lower item×model interaction)。这是一个honest limitation， 不fatal但weakens generalizability claims。\\n\\n\\nexp-005有两个blocking依赖：(1) exp-003需要先完成以确定multi-hardware protocol； (2) 14B模型需要≥48GB VRAM GPU(RTX PRO 6000的96GB可以跑，但如果被exp-001/002 占用则无法parallel执行)。在worst档中exp-003未完成，且GPU时间被P0实验消耗， P1实验无法启动。这意味着论文没有model scale的evidence——所有结论限于7-9B模型。 reviewer concern \\\"Missing scale validation\\\"完全无法回应。\\n\"\n885\t  },\n886\t  {\n887\t    \"name\": \"exp-006: Fine-tuning G-study\",\n888\t    \"description\": \"RoBERTa-large在SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA上的fine-tuning方差分解。 在worst档中未执行——P1优先级，GPU时间不足。\\n\",\n889\t    \"results\": {\n890\t      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n891\t      \"blocking_reason\": \"P1 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\",\n892\t      \"records_collected\": 0\n893\t    },\n894\t    \"tables\": [],\n895\t    \"figures\": [],\n896\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-006缺失不fatal。论文可以在Future Work中提到\\\"extending G-theory to fine-tuning variance decomposition\\\"作为natural extension。reviewer concern #5 \\\"No training-time analysis\\\"的回应是\\\"this paper focuses on inference-time evaluation reliability; fine-tuning stability is a complementary question studied by Dodge et al. (2020) and Sellam et al. (2022)\\\"。\\n\\n\\nexp-006需要1,728次fine-tuning runs × ~15min each ≈ 430 GPU-hours。在worst档中 P0实验消耗了大部分GPU预算(exp-001 ~100h实际可能超支至130h due to reruns， exp-002 ~80h for partial completion)，剩余GPU时间不足以执行P1实验。 此外，exp-006是encoder-only(RoBERTa) fine-tuning，与主论文的decoder LLM inference focus有methodological gap——即使完成，reviewer可能argue \\\"fine-tuning variance structure is a different question\\\"。在worst档的reframed narrative中(focus on metric-conditional reliability)，fine-tuning分析不是 核心贡献，缺失的impact有限。\\n\"\n897\t  },\n898\t  {\n899\t    \"name\": \"exp-007: LayerCast precision mitigation\",\n900\t    \"description\": \"LayerCast(FP16 storage + FP32 compute)作为额外precision条件。 P2优先级，在worst档中未执行。\\n\",\n901\t    \"results\": {\n902\t      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n903\t      \"blocking_reason\": \"P2 priority; not attempted within timeline\",\n904\t      \"records_collected\": 0\n905\t    },\n906\t    \"tables\": [],\n907\t    \"figures\": [],\n908\t    \"analysis\": \"零影响。LayerCast从未进入任何版本的核心claims。\\n\\n\\nP2实验在worst档中完全被deprioritize。LayerCast是一个mitigation strategy的 evaluation——属于\\\"nice to have\\\"而非核心贡献。论文可以在Discussion中提到 LayerCast作为\\\"practitioners can reduce precision-related variance via mixed precision strategies\\\"的建议，无需实验数据支撑(cite existing LayerCast papers)。\\n\"\n909\t  },\n910\t  {\n911\t    \"name\": \"exp-008: MATH benchmark\",\n912\t    \"description\": \"MATH benchmark(200 items)测试reasoning-heavy任务的item dominance。 P2优先级且deps on exp-002，在worst档中未执行。\\n\",\n913\t    \"results\": {\n914\t      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n915\t      \"blocking_reason\": \"P2 priority; depends on exp-002 (incomplete)\",\n916\t      \"records_collected\": 0\n917\t    },\n918\t    \"tables\": [],\n919\t    \"figures\": [],\n920\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-008缺失加剧了\\\"primarily MMLU\\\"的scope limitation，但由于worst档已放弃 cross-benchmark gradient叙事，这个实验的缺失与narrative adjustment是consistent的。\\n\\n\\nexp-008依赖exp-002的multi-benchmark infrastructure和protocol。exp-002本身 在worst档中仅部分完成，exp-008作为P2自然被skip。MATH作为reasoning benchmark 可能会显示更低的item_id(类似GSM8K的38%)和更高的residual，但没有数据只能 在Discussion中speculate。reviewer concern \\\"Only MMLU\\\"的回应依赖GSM8K的 部分数据(\\\"we have preliminary evidence from GSM8K suggesting task-type dependence\\\")。\\n\"\n921\t  }\n922\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df06a013-e402-44e4-948d-a7387355c397","uuid":"efbfc2ef-64f1-44b5-84e7-c70d95692582","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:39:25.421Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044739/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-001: Full-scale 4-model MMLU precision study\",\n    \"description\": \"4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temps × 4 orderings × 200 items。 Llama/Gemma已完成，Mistral完成，Qwen3完成但有部分数据质量问题(~3%条件因OOM重跑， 引入时间混淆)。4-model G-theory分析揭示2-model结果被Llama/Gemma架构差异放大。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_records\": 825600,\n      \"total_conditions\": 5184,\n      \"models_completed\": 4,\n      \"qwen3_data_quality_issues_pct\": 3.2,\n      \"llama_single_model_item_id_pct\": 61.1,\n      \"gemma_single_model_item_id_pct\": 57.3,\n      \"mistral_single_model_item_id_pct\": 59.8,\n      \"qwen3_single_model_item_id_pct\": 55.2,\n      \"avg_single_model_item_id_pct\": 58.4,\n      \"avg_single_model_G_binary\": 0.594,\n      \"cross_4model_item_id_pct\": 42.1,\n      \"cross_4model_model_x_item_pct\": 7.3,\n      \"cross_4model_model_pct\": 18.6,\n      \"cross_4model_residual_pct\": 19.8,\n      \"cross_4model_prompt_x_item_pct\": 4.2,\n      \"cross_4model_seed_x_item_pct\": 3.9,\n      \"cross_4model_temp_pct\": 1.1,\n      \"cross_4model_precision_pct\": 0.8,\n      \"cross_4model_G_binary\": 0.31,\n      \"two_model_model_x_item_pct\": 26.2,\n      \"four_model_model_x_item_pct\": 7.3,\n      \"interaction_decay_ratio\": 3.59,\n      \"two_model_cross_G\": 0.073,\n      \"four_model_cross_G\": 0.31,\n      \"infra_x_method_binary_pct\": 0.3,\n      \"infra_x_method_text_pct\": 3.8,\n      \"precision_x_temperature_text_pct\": 4.15,\n      \"llama_bf16_fp16_gap_pp\": 0.06,\n      \"gemma_bf16_fp32_gap_pp\": 7.8,\n      \"mistral_bf16_fp16_gap_pp\": 0.09,\n      \"qwen3_bf16_fp16_gap_pp\": 0.12,\n      \"fp32_degradation_all_models_avg_pp\": 7.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: Single-model variance decomposition (4 models separately)\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"source\",\n            \"llama_pct\",\n            \"gemma_pct\",\n            \"mistral_pct\",\n            \"qwen3_pct\",\n            \"avg_pct\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"item_id\",\n              61.1,\n              57.3,\n              59.8,\n              55.2,\n              58.4\n            ],\n            [\n              \"residual\",\n              17.2,\n              19.8,\n              18.6,\n              21.3,\n              19.2\n            ],\n            [\n              \"prompt_template:item_id\",\n              4.6,\n              5.1,\n              4.8,\n              5.9,\n              5.1\n            ],\n            [\n              \"seed:item_id\",\n              4.7,\n              4.3,\n              4.5,\n              5.2,\n              4.7\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision:item_id\",\n              7.2,\n              8.1,\n              6.9,\n              6.3,\n              7.1\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature:item_id\",\n              3.1,\n              3.5,\n              3.2,\n              3.8,\n              3.4\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature\",\n              1.2,\n              1.0,\n              1.4,\n              1.1,\n              1.2\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision\",\n              0.5,\n              0.4,\n              0.3,\n              0.7,\n              0.5\n            ],\n            [\n              \"other_interactions\",\n              0.4,\n              0.5,\n              0.5,\n              0.5,\n              0.5\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: 2-model vs 4-model crossed variance decomposition\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"source\",\n            \"two_model_pct\",\n            \"four_model_pct\",\n            \"delta_pp\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"item_id\",\n              37.9,\n              42.1,\n              4.2\n            ],\n            [\n              \"model_x_item\",\n              26.2,\n              7.3,\n              -18.9\n            ],\n            [\n              \"model\",\n              0.8,\n              18.6,\n              17.8\n            ],\n            [\n              \"residual\",\n              21.6,\n              19.8,\n              -1.8\n            ],\n            [\n              \"prompt_template:item_id\",\n              4.6,\n              4.2,\n              -0.4\n            ],\n            [\n              \"seed:item_id\",\n              4.7,\n              3.9,\n              -0.8\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision:item_id\",\n              2.8,\n              2.4,\n              -0.4\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature\",\n              0.9,\n              1.1,\n              0.2\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision\",\n              0.3,\n              0.8,\n              0.5\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: G-coefficients comparison\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"design\",\n            \"metric\",\n            \"G_coeff\",\n            \"se\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"single_model_avg\",\n              \"binary\",\n              0.594,\n              0.032\n            ],\n            [\n              \"single_model_avg\",\n              \"text_exact\",\n              0.008,\n              0.003\n            ],\n            [\n              \"2_model_crossed\",\n              \"binary\",\n              0.073,\n              0.018\n            ],\n            [\n              \"4_model_crossed\",\n              \"binary\",\n              0.31,\n              0.041\n            ],\n            [\n              \"4_model_crossed\",\n              \"text_exact\",\n              0.005,\n              0.002\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"4-model结果对原Claim 1构成实质性削弱。model×item从26.2%降至7.3%意味着这不再是 \\\"second-largest source\\\"——它现在排在item_id(42.1%)、residual(19.8%)、model(18.6%) 之后，仅列第四。cross-model G从0.073升至0.31意味着\\\"崩塌\\\"叙事需要大幅修正。 但0.31仍远低于0.80，model-specificity仍然是一个真实现象。论文策略：将model×item 降级为\\\"preliminary evidence\\\"，报告2-model和4-model结果，讨论sample size对 interaction estimation的影响(2-model只有1 df for model)，标注需要8+模型验证。 转而将metric-conditional G差异(binary 0.594 vs text 0.008)和IRT对比作为headline。 单模型方差分解的稳定性(4模型间item_id=55-61%)是一个正面发现——可以支撑 \\\"item dominance is robust across models\\\"的claim，但这是psychometrically expected 而非novel。\\n\\n\\n2-model设计中26.2%的model×item交互源于Llama(decoder-only, GQA)与Gemma(decoder-only, grouped-query attention, 不同tokenizer)之间的架构最大化差异：两者在知识编码方式上 根本不同，导致同一item对Llama容易而对Gemma难（或反之）。加入Mistral(与Llama架构 高度同源，同为Llama-family decoder)和Qwen3(类似架构但中文语料占比高)后， model facet的universe从\\\"两个极端\\\"扩展到\\\"四个含重叠的点\\\"。Mistral/Qwen3的item difficulty profile与Llama高度相关(r>0.85)，稀释了Gemma带来的交互方差。 统计上，2-model设计中model只有1个自由度，任何两模型间的系统差异都被完全分配到 model×item交互项；4-model设计中model有3个自由度，交互被分散。model主效应从<1% 升至18.6%反映了模型间整体能力差异(Gemma 73.2% vs Llama 66.4% vs Mistral 62.1% vs Qwen3 68.5%)在4-model设计中被正确分离。cross-model G从0.073回升至0.31意味着 item difficulty rankings在4模型间有moderate一致性——\\\"崩塌\\\"叙事不成立，但0.31仍 远低于0.80的dependability阈值，model-specificity是真实存在的，只是程度被2-model 设计夸大了约3.6倍。infra×method在binary下仍<0.5%，text-exact-match下precision× temperature仍~4%，与Llama-only结果一致——这一finding是robust的。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-001b: Item resampling stability test\",\n    \"description\": \"3个独立200-item分层抽样(seeds 0, 7, 99) × Llama × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54条件/样本。测试item_id dominance是否依赖具体抽样。 在worst档中，resampling显示item_id有显著波动，200-item子集不够stable。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_samples\": 3,\n      \"conditions_per_sample\": 54,\n      \"records_per_sample\": 10800,\n      \"total_records\": 32400,\n      \"sample_seed0_item_id_pct\": 61.1,\n      \"sample_seed7_item_id_pct\": 52.4,\n      \"sample_seed99_item_id_pct\": 68.3,\n      \"item_id_mean_pct\": 60.6,\n      \"item_id_sd_pct\": 8.0,\n      \"item_id_cv\": 0.132,\n      \"item_id_cv_strict\": 0.3,\n      \"sample_seed0_G_binary\": 0.611,\n      \"sample_seed7_G_binary\": 0.528,\n      \"sample_seed99_G_binary\": 0.697,\n      \"G_binary_mean\": 0.612,\n      \"G_binary_sd\": 0.085,\n      \"G_binary_cv\": 0.139,\n      \"variance_structure_profile_corr_01\": 0.82,\n      \"variance_structure_profile_corr_02\": 0.71,\n      \"variance_structure_profile_corr_12\": 0.77,\n      \"avg_profile_corr\": 0.77,\n      \"sample_seed0_residual_pct\": 17.2,\n      \"sample_seed7_residual_pct\": 22.8,\n      \"sample_seed99_residual_pct\": 13.1,\n      \"residual_cv\": 0.28,\n      \"resampling_item_id_cv\": 0.3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Resampling stability across 3 item draws\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"source\",\n            \"seed0_pct\",\n            \"seed7_pct\",\n            \"seed99_pct\",\n            \"mean_pct\",\n            \"sd_pct\",\n            \"cv\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"item_id\",\n              61.1,\n              52.4,\n              68.3,\n              60.6,\n              8.0,\n              0.3\n            ],\n            [\n              \"residual\",\n              17.2,\n              22.8,\n              13.1,\n              17.7,\n              4.9,\n              0.28\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision:item_id\",\n              7.2,\n              8.5,\n              5.8,\n              7.2,\n              1.4,\n              0.19\n            ],\n            [\n              \"prompt_template:item_id\",\n              4.6,\n              5.9,\n              4.1,\n              4.9,\n              0.9,\n              0.19\n            ],\n            [\n              \"seed:item_id\",\n              4.7,\n              5.1,\n              4.3,\n              4.7,\n              0.4,\n              0.09\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature:item_id\",\n              3.1,\n              3.2,\n              2.9,\n              3.1,\n              0.2,\n              0.05\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature\",\n              1.2,\n              1.1,\n              1.0,\n              1.1,\n              0.1,\n              0.09\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision\",\n              0.5,\n              0.6,\n              0.3,\n              0.5,\n              0.2,\n              0.33\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: G-coefficient stability\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"metric\",\n            \"seed0_G\",\n            \"seed7_G\",\n            \"seed99_G\",\n            \"mean_G\",\n            \"sd_G\",\n            \"cv\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"binary\",\n              0.611,\n              0.528,\n              0.697,\n              0.612,\n              0.085,\n              0.139\n            ],\n            [\n              \"text_exact\",\n              0.007,\n              0.005,\n              0.009,\n              0.007,\n              0.002,\n              0.286\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"resampling结果对论文有双重影响。负面：具体百分比不可作为precise point estimate， 需要报告range或bootstrap CI，reviewer可以合理质疑\\\"为什么不用更大的item sample\\\"。 正面：item_id在所有3次抽样中都是最大的方差源(最低52.4%仍远超第二名residual的 22.8%)，定性结论robust。G系数的较低变异(CV=0.14)意味着reliability estimate 本身是stable的——即使方差成分百分比波动，最终的reliability判断不变。 论文策略：报告mean±SD而非point estimate，在Limitations中讨论200-item的局限性， 引用D-study中500+item的建议作为methodological回应。但这会让数字less punchy， reviewer可能觉得\\\"连自己的item sample都不stable，怎么能给别人开D-study budgets\\\"。\\n\\n\\n200 items是MMLU 14,042题的1.4%。即使使用分层抽样(按subject category)，每个category 内部的item difficulty分布仍有大量变异。MMLU的57个subjects中，部分subjects只有 ~100题，200-item分层抽样在小subjects上可能只抽到2-3题，这些题的难度方差极大。 seed 99的样本恰好抽到了更多高方差subjects(如abstract_algebra, formal_logic)中的 极端难度item，导致item_id膨胀至68.3%——这些item的p值接近0或1，Bernoulli方差 p(1-p)很小，但它们与其他item的巨大difficulty差异推高了item_id方差成分。 seed 7的样本则抽到了更\\\"中等难度\\\"的集中分布，item间差异较小，item_id降至52.4%。 CV=0.30意味着200-item子集的方差结构有约30%的相对变异——这不是catastrophic (方差ranking仍然是item_id > residual > others)，但足以让reviewer质疑具体百分比 的精确性。对论文的影响：不能把\\\"61.1%\\\"当作point estimate来报告，需要报告 range [52-68%]或CI。这削弱了精确数字的说服力，但\\\"item dominance\\\"的定性结论 (item_id始终是最大source)仍然成立。G系数的CV=0.14较低，说明reliability estimate本身比方差百分比更robust——这是一个可以强调的正面发现。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-002: Multi-benchmark replication (partial)\",\n    \"description\": \"原计划4 models × 2 prec × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings × 200 items on GSM8K/ARC/HellaSwag。在worst档中，仅完成GSM8K(1个模型完整 + 1个模型部分)和ARC-Challenge(1个模型部分)。HellaSwag未开始。 时间被exp-001的4-model数据收集和debug消耗。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gsm8k_models_completed\": 1.5,\n      \"arc_models_completed\": 0.7,\n      \"hellaswag_models_completed\": 0,\n      \"total_benchmarks_with_usable_data\": 2,\n      \"total_benchmarks_fully_completed\": 0,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_records\": 57600,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_conditions\": 288,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_item_id_pct\": 38.2,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_residual_pct\": 31.4,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_prompt_template_item_id_pct\": 12.8,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_seed_item_id_pct\": 6.1,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_temperature_pct\": 5.3,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_precision_item_id_pct\": 2.9,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_precision_pct\": 0.4,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_G_binary\": 0.421,\n      \"gsm8k_llama_overall_accuracy\": 0.487,\n      \"arc_llama_records\": 40320,\n      \"arc_llama_conditions\": 202,\n      \"arc_llama_item_id_pct\": 54.7,\n      \"arc_llama_residual_pct\": 20.3,\n      \"arc_llama_prompt_template_item_id_pct\": 7.2,\n      \"arc_llama_G_binary\": 0.563,\n      \"arc_llama_overall_accuracy\": 0.531,\n      \"arc_data_quality_note\": \"partial, missing T=0.7 conditions and orderings 3-4\",\n      \"mmlu_item_id_pct\": 61.1,\n      \"gsm8k_item_id_pct\": 38.2,\n      \"arc_item_id_pct\": 54.7,\n      \"benchmark_item_id_spread_pp\": 22.9,\n      \"benchmark_gradient_rank_corr\": 0.0,\n      \"mmlu_gsm8k_profile_corr\": 0.42,\n      \"mmlu_arc_profile_corr\": 0.78,\n      \"gsm8k_arc_profile_corr\": 0.38,\n      \"gsm8k_infra_x_method_binary_pct\": 1.1,\n      \"arc_infra_x_method_binary_pct\": 0.6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Cross-benchmark variance decomposition (single-model Llama, binary)\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"source\",\n            \"mmlu_pct\",\n            \"gsm8k_pct\",\n            \"arc_partial_pct\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"item_id\",\n              61.1,\n              38.2,\n              54.7\n            ],\n            [\n              \"residual\",\n              17.2,\n              31.4,\n              20.3\n            ],\n            [\n              \"prompt_template:item_id\",\n              4.6,\n              12.8,\n              7.2\n            ],\n            [\n              \"seed:item_id\",\n              4.7,\n              6.1,\n              5.8\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature\",\n              1.2,\n              5.3,\n              3.1\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision:item_id\",\n              7.2,\n              2.9,\n              5.2\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature:item_id\",\n              3.1,\n              1.8,\n              2.4\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision\",\n              0.5,\n              0.4,\n              0.3\n            ],\n            [\n              \"other\",\n              0.4,\n              1.1,\n              1.0\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: G-coefficients by benchmark\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"benchmark\",\n            \"n_items\",\n            \"G_binary\",\n            \"G_text_exact\",\n            \"data_completeness\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"MMLU\",\n              200,\n              0.611,\n              0.007,\n              \"100%\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"GSM8K\",\n              200,\n              0.421,\n              0.003,\n              \"100% (1 model)\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"ARC-Challenge\",\n              200,\n              0.563,\n              0.006,\n              \"~70% (1 model)\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"HellaSwag\",\n              200,\n              null,\n              null,\n              \"0%\"\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-002的部分完成是worst档的核心打击之一。原计划的\\\"cross-benchmark gradient\\\" 需要4+ benchmarks才能做credible的趋势分析，现在只有1.5-2个可用benchmark。 MMLU 61% vs GSM8K 38%的差异是real的，但2个数据点无法支撑\\\"gradient\\\"叙事—— reviewer会问\\\"这只是两个不同benchmark有不同方差结构，这不是trivially obvious吗？\\\" ARC的部分数据(54.7%)暗示MMLU和GSM8K之间可能有中间值，但数据不完整不能claim。 论文策略：完全放弃\\\"cross-benchmark gradient\\\"作为finding，改为在Discussion中 报告MMLU vs GSM8K的差异作为\\\"preliminary observation suggesting task-type dependence\\\"。GSM8K的数据可以用来支持\\\"metric-conditional\\\"叙事——GSM8K的 G=0.421(vs MMLU 0.611)意味着reasoning benchmarks需要更多items，但这是 a secondary point。主要损失：论文scope从\\\"multi-benchmark\\\"收缩为\\\"primarily MMLU with limited cross-benchmark evidence\\\"。\\n\\n\\nGSM8K是数学推理任务，与MMLU的知识检索本质不同。GSM8K的item_id仅38.2%(vs MMLU 61.1%)的原因：数学题的难度方差较小——大多数GSM8K题都需要类似步骤数的算术推理， item间difficulty差异不如MMLU(从trivia到advanced physics)那么大。相应地， prompt_template:item_id升至12.8%，因为chain-of-thought prompt的具体措辞对 推理过程影响更大(某些prompt引导更好的step-by-step分解)。residual升至31.4% 反映了推理任务的内在随机性——同一条件下模型可能走不同推理路径得到不同结果。 temperature对GSM8K的影响更大(5.3% vs MMLU 1.2%)因为推理任务在T>0时的token sampling对中间步骤的影响会cascade到最终答案。ARC-Challenge(科学推理)的item_id 54.7%介于MMLU和GSM8K之间，但仅有部分数据不能做firm conclusion。 关键问题：3个数据点(MMLU 61%, ARC 55%, GSM8K 38%)没有形成任何可解释的梯度—— 按\\\"知识→推理\\\"排序是MMLU>ARC>GSM8K，看起来有趋势但(a)ARC也是部分数据， (b)HellaSwag缺失，(c) 3个点无法区分线性趋势和噪声(任何3点都可以拟合线性)。 Rank correlation无法计算或为trivially 1.0(3个点完美排序)，无统计意义。 infra×method在GSM8K上为1.1%(高于MMLU的<0.1%)，可能反映precision对推理中间 步骤的累积效应，但也可能是noise——CI会很宽。这个结果让\\\"separability is benchmark-conditional\\\"无法作为firm claim。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-003: Multi-hardware G-study\",\n    \"description\": \"原计划4 models × 2+ GPU types × 2 precisions × full crossing on MMLU。 在worst档中，exp-003完全缺失——第二种GPU类型从未获得。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n      \"gpu_types_available\": 1,\n      \"second_gpu_acquired\": false,\n      \"records_collected\": 0,\n      \"blocking_reason\": \"second GPU type not procured; Manager unable to allocate additional hardware within timeline\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-003缺失是worst档的defining characteristic之一。impact chain: (1) Claim 2的\\\"infrastructure\\\"缩窄为\\\"precision\\\"； (2) exp-004的hardware分析完全消失； (3) exp-005被block； (4) \\\"fully crossed infrastructure × methodology\\\"的framing需要大幅qualifier； (5) 论文title如果含\\\"hardware\\\"需要修改。 Mitigation：将exp-003的缺失作为explicit limitation，引用Yuan et al. (2025)的 hardware结果作为motivation for future work，强调我们的precision分析是 complementary而非替代。在rebuttal策略中准备\\\"we agree hardware crossing is essential and plan to include it in camera-ready\\\"（如果能在rebuttal期间拿到GPU）。\\n\\n\\nexp-003的核心依赖是获得第二种GPU类型(如A100、H100或不同架构的consumer GPU)。 在worst档中，Manager未能在AAAI截稿前分配额外硬件——可能因预算审批延迟、 GPU集群排队过长、或所需硬件型号不可用。没有第二种GPU，\\\"multi-hardware crossing\\\" 无法执行。论文只能在单一GPU(RTX PRO 6000)上讨论\\\"numerical precision\\\"而非 \\\"hardware\\\"。这意味着Claim 2(Conditional Separability)中的\\\"infrastructure\\\" 实际上仅限于\\\"precision facet on a single GPU architecture\\\"——reviewer会指出 这是一个substantial scope limitation。Yuan et al. (2025)记录的hardware-specific 差异(CUDA kernel implementation, memory bandwidth effects)完全无法验证。 但论文可以将此framing为\\\"precision is the infrastructure facet we can study; hardware crossing is future work\\\"——这是honest但weakens the contribution。 exp-003的缺失也影响exp-004(无hardware数据可分析)和exp-005(deps on exp-003)。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-004: Comprehensive analysis (partial)\",\n    \"description\": \"基于exp-001/001b/002/003数据的全面分析。在worst档中，缺失exp-003数据， exp-002仅有部分数据。分析范围大幅收缩。仍可执行的分析： (a) 4-model G-theory交叉分析(exp-001)，(b) bootstrap CIs，(c) IRT对比， (d) 温度分层，(e) 固定vs随机敏感性，(f) D-study投影。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"planned_analyses\": 17,\n      \"executable_analyses\": 11,\n      \"blocked_analyses\": 6,\n      \"irt_recommended_items_mmlu\": 142,\n      \"gtheory_recommended_items_mmlu_practical\": 468,\n      \"gtheory_recommended_items_mmlu_all_random\": 498,\n      \"irt_vs_gtheory_item_ratio_practical\": 3.3,\n      \"irt_vs_gtheory_item_ratio_all_random\": 3.51,\n      \"irt_gtheory_gap_attribution\": {\n        \"prompt_x_item_contribution_pct\": 32,\n        \"seed_x_item_contribution_pct\": 28,\n        \"ordering_residual_contribution_pct\": 40\n      },\n      \"item_id_pct_CI_95\": [\n        56.8,\n        65.4\n      ],\n      \"model_x_item_4model_pct_CI_95\": [\n        3.1,\n        12.8\n      ],\n      \"residual_pct_CI_95\": [\n        15.3,\n        23.1\n      ],\n      \"G_binary_CI_95\": [\n        0.571,\n        0.652\n      ],\n      \"cross_model_G_4model_CI_95\": [\n        0.22,\n        0.4\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_width_ratio_mean\": 1.01,\n      \"bootstrap_jackknife_CI_width_ratio_range\": [\n        0.96,\n        1.04\n      ],\n      \"G_all_random\": 0.594,\n      \"G_temp_fixed\": 0.628,\n      \"G_ordering_fixed\": 0.641,\n      \"G_both_fixed\": 0.664,\n      \"fixed_random_max_delta_G\": 0.07,\n      \"d_study_items_G80_practical\": 468,\n      \"d_study_items_G80_all_random\": 498,\n      \"d_study_items_G90_practical\": 1240,\n      \"d_study_items_G90_all_random\": 1380,\n      \"d_study_14k_items_G_projected\": 0.972,\n      \"bernoulli_variance_obs_variance_corr\": 0.91,\n      \"null_sim_item_id_pct\": 0.08,\n      \"obs_bernoulli_ratio\": 1.0008,\n      \"min_detectable_interaction_pct\": 0.8,\n      \"power_at_1pct_interaction\": 0.94,\n      \"power_at_5pct_interaction\": 0.99,\n      \"gpu_hours_for_G80_budget\": 340,\n      \"gpu_hours_for_G90_budget\": 890\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: IRT vs G-theory item requirements comparison\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"method\",\n            \"framework\",\n            \"recommended_items\",\n            \"target\",\n            \"accounts_for\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"IRT_2PL\",\n              \"IRT\",\n              142,\n              \"SE<0.3\",\n              \"item difficulty + discrimination\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"G_practical\",\n              \"G-theory\",\n              468,\n              \"G≥0.80\",\n              \"item + prompt + seed + ordering (temp/prec fixed)\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"G_all_random\",\n              \"G-theory\",\n              498,\n              \"G≥0.80\",\n              \"item + prompt + seed + ordering + temp + prec\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"G_high_rel\",\n              \"G-theory\",\n              1240,\n              \"G≥0.90\",\n              \"item + prompt + seed + ordering (temp/prec fixed)\"\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Bootstrap CIs on 4-model variance components\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"source\",\n            \"point_est_pct\",\n            \"ci_lower\",\n            \"ci_upper\",\n            \"ci_width\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"item_id\",\n              42.1,\n              36.5,\n              47.8,\n              11.3\n            ],\n            [\n              \"model\",\n              18.6,\n              12.1,\n              25.4,\n              13.3\n            ],\n            [\n              \"residual\",\n              19.8,\n              15.3,\n              23.1,\n              7.8\n            ],\n            [\n              \"model_x_item\",\n              7.3,\n              3.1,\n              12.8,\n              9.7\n            ],\n            [\n              \"prompt_x_item\",\n              4.2,\n              2.8,\n              5.9,\n              3.1\n            ],\n            [\n              \"seed_x_item\",\n              3.9,\n              2.5,\n              5.6,\n              3.1\n            ],\n            [\n              \"precision_x_item\",\n              2.4,\n              1.1,\n              4.0,\n              2.9\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temperature\",\n              1.1,\n              0.4,\n              2.2,\n              1.8\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: D-study projections (4-model, binary metric)\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"design\",\n            \"n_items\",\n            \"n_prompts\",\n            \"n_seeds\",\n            \"G_projected\",\n            \"ci_95\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"current_practice\",\n              200,\n              1,\n              1,\n              0.594,\n              \"[0.571, 0.652]\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"practical_G80\",\n              468,\n              2,\n              3,\n              0.801,\n              \"[0.782, 0.831]\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"all_random_G80\",\n              498,\n              2,\n              3,\n              0.8,\n              \"[0.778, 0.825]\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"practical_G90\",\n              1240,\n              3,\n              4,\n              0.901,\n              \"[0.887, 0.918]\"\n            ],\n            [\n              \"full_mmlu\",\n              14042,\n              6,\n              6,\n              0.972,\n              \"[0.968, 0.979]\"\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Fixed vs random facet sensitivity (MD2)\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"configuration\",\n            \"G_binary\",\n            \"delta_from_all_random\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"all_random\",\n              0.594,\n              0.0\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temp_fixed\",\n              0.628,\n              0.034\n            ],\n            [\n              \"ordering_fixed\",\n              0.641,\n              0.047\n            ],\n            [\n              \"temp+ordering_fixed\",\n              0.664,\n              0.07\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Bootstrap vs Jackknife CI comparison (MD6)\",\n        \"data\": {\n          \"columns\": [\n            \"component\",\n            \"bootstrap_ci_width\",\n            \"jackknife_ci_width\",\n            \"ratio\"\n          ],\n          \"rows\": [\n            [\n              \"item_id\",\n              8.6,\n              8.4,\n              1.02\n            ],\n            [\n              \"residual\",\n              7.8,\n              8.1,\n              0.96\n            ],\n            [\n              \"model_x_item\",\n              9.7,\n              9.4,\n              1.03\n            ],\n            [\n              \"prompt_x_item\",\n              3.1,\n              3.0,\n              1.03\n            ],\n            [\n              \"G_binary\",\n              0.081,\n              0.079,\n              1.03\n            ]\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-004在worst档中的核心价值：IRT对比(3.3×)和方法学robustness package。 IRT对比是self-contained的——不需要4-model结果或cross-benchmark数据，完全基于 已完成的单模型Llama MMLU数据。3.3×的ratio比原预期低，但仍是\\\"首个G-theory vs IRT直接对比\\\"的文献贡献。Bootstrap CIs的宽度(model×item CI包含接近0的值)进一步 支持将model×item降级为\\\"preliminary\\\"的策略。方法学robustness的6项验证全部 基于exp-001 Llama数据，不受worst档的数据缺失影响——这是论文的\\\"guaranteed floor\\\"。 Missing analyses: hardware crossing(exp-003缺失)、full cross-benchmark gradient (exp-002不完整)、full logprob analysis across benchmarks(只能做MMLU)。 论文需要在Methods中明确标注哪些分析是\\\"planned but not completed\\\"。\\n\\n\\nIRT vs G-theory对比是worst档中最重要的surviving finding。ratio降至3.3-3.5× (vs原预期的3.6-4.4×)因为：(1) IRT在4-model数据上估计更保守(142 items vs 原2-model数据的116 items)，缩小了分母差距；(2) G-theory的D-study在4-model 分析中因model×item降至7.3%而需要fewer items来compensate，numerator从510 降至498。但3.3×仍然是substantial——意味着IRT忽略的facets(prompt, seed, ordering) 贡献了约70%的额外item需求。这个finding不依赖model×item的magnitude，因此 在worst档中仍然robust。 Bootstrap CIs显示item_id的95% CI为[56.8, 65.4]，range=8.6pp——与exp-001b的 resampling变异一致，进一步确认point estimate的精度有限。model×item的4-model CI为[3.1, 12.8]，range很宽(9.7pp)，包含0附近——reviewer可能argue \\\"model×item is not reliably different from zero with only 4 models\\\"。这是对原Claim 1的 又一打击：不仅point estimate从26%降至7%，CI还包含了trivially small values。 Fixed vs random sensitivity分析显示delta仅0.070——这是一个正面结果，说明 facet classification choice对G影响不大，MD2 defense成立。 D-study投影到14K items给出G=0.972——这回应了MD5 \\\"MMLU is unreliable\\\"的误读， 但468 items for G≥0.80(vs原文510)的变化需要更新所有D-study tables。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-005: 14B model extension\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen3-14B和DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B在datacenter GPU上的BF16/FP16测试。 在worst档中，因exp-003缺失(deps)且无可用的48GB+ VRAM GPU而完全未执行。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n      \"blocking_reason\": \"Depends on exp-003 (not completed); no 48GB+ VRAM GPU available\",\n      \"records_collected\": 0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-005缺失意味着reviewer concern #6 \\\"Missing scale validation\\\"只能在Limitations 中acknowledge。论文需要argue \\\"7-9B models represent the most commonly used open-weight model class\\\"并cite usage statistics。但sophisticated reviewer会 指出14B+模型可能有qualitatively different variance structure(更稳定的internal representations → lower item×model interaction)。这是一个honest limitation， 不fatal但weakens generalizability claims。\\n\\n\\nexp-005有两个blocking依赖：(1) exp-003需要先完成以确定multi-hardware protocol； (2) 14B模型需要≥48GB VRAM GPU(RTX PRO 6000的96GB可以跑，但如果被exp-001/002 占用则无法parallel执行)。在worst档中exp-003未完成，且GPU时间被P0实验消耗， P1实验无法启动。这意味着论文没有model scale的evidence——所有结论限于7-9B模型。 reviewer concern \\\"Missing scale validation\\\"完全无法回应。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-006: Fine-tuning G-study\",\n    \"description\": \"RoBERTa-large在SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA上的fine-tuning方差分解。 在worst档中未执行——P1优先级，GPU时间不足。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n      \"blocking_reason\": \"P1 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\",\n      \"records_collected\": 0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-006缺失不fatal。论文可以在Future Work中提到\\\"extending G-theory to fine-tuning variance decomposition\\\"作为natural extension。reviewer concern #5 \\\"No training-time analysis\\\"的回应是\\\"this paper focuses on inference-time evaluation reliability; fine-tuning stability is a complementary question studied by Dodge et al. (2020) and Sellam et al. (2022)\\\"。\\n\\n\\nexp-006需要1,728次fine-tuning runs × ~15min each ≈ 430 GPU-hours。在worst档中 P0实验消耗了大部分GPU预算(exp-001 ~100h实际可能超支至130h due to reruns， exp-002 ~80h for partial completion)，剩余GPU时间不足以执行P1实验。 此外，exp-006是encoder-only(RoBERTa) fine-tuning，与主论文的decoder LLM inference focus有methodological gap——即使完成，reviewer可能argue \\\"fine-tuning variance structure is a different question\\\"。在worst档的reframed narrative中(focus on metric-conditional reliability)，fine-tuning分析不是 核心贡献，缺失的impact有限。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-007: LayerCast precision mitigation\",\n    \"description\": \"LayerCast(FP16 storage + FP32 compute)作为额外precision条件。 P2优先级，在worst档中未执行。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n      \"blocking_reason\": \"P2 priority; not attempted within timeline\",\n      \"records_collected\": 0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"零影响。LayerCast从未进入任何版本的核心claims。\\n\\n\\nP2实验在worst档中完全被deprioritize。LayerCast是一个mitigation strategy的 evaluation——属于\\\"nice to have\\\"而非核心贡献。论文可以在Discussion中提到 LayerCast作为\\\"practitioners can reduce precision-related variance via mixed precision strategies\\\"的建议，无需实验数据支撑(cite existing LayerCast papers)。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exp-008: MATH benchmark\",\n    \"description\": \"MATH benchmark(200 items)测试reasoning-heavy任务的item dominance。 P2优先级且deps on exp-002，在worst档中未执行。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"NOT_COMPLETED\",\n      \"blocking_reason\": \"P2 priority; depends on exp-002 (incomplete)\",\n      \"records_collected\": 0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-008缺失加剧了\\\"primarily MMLU\\\"的scope limitation，但由于worst档已放弃 cross-benchmark gradient叙事，这个实验的缺失与narrative adjustment是consistent的。\\n\\n\\nexp-008依赖exp-002的multi-benchmark infrastructure和protocol。exp-002本身 在worst档中仅部分完成，exp-008作为P2自然被skip。MATH作为reasoning benchmark 可能会显示更低的item_id(类似GSM8K的38%)和更高的residual，但没有数据只能 在Discussion中speculate。reviewer concern \\\"Only MMLU\\\"的回应依赖GSM8K的 部分数据(\\\"we have preliminary evidence from GSM8K suggesting task-type dependence\\\")。\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":922,"startLine":1,"totalLines":922}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdHUMb345FwEcWuFujdqMW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n5\t- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n6\t- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n7\t\n8\t## Title\n9\tReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Model-Conditional Item Dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in single-model LLM evaluation (61.1% on MMLU), but model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest source when model is treated as a crossed facet — collapsing cross-model generalizability (G: 0.611→0.073). Item difficulty rankings are model-specific: two models with similar aggregate accuracy (Llama 66.4% vs Gemma 73.2%) disagree dramatically at the item level. This means single-model variance decompositions provide a false sense of reliability. Cross-benchmark gradient (pending exp-002) is hypothesized to further modulate this structure.\n14\t**Empirical anchors (exp-001, 2 models, 325,600 records)**: Single-model item_id=61.1%, cross-model item_id=37.9% (↓23pp), model×item=26.2%, cross-model G=0.073. D-study: even 10 models yield G=0.179 — model×item interaction is irreducible by adding models.\n15\t**Fallback narratives**: (A) If 4-model analysis confirms 2-model trend → \"model×item interaction is a fundamental barrier to cross-model generalizability\"; (B) If 4-model analysis shows stabilization (item_id rebounds) → \"2-model analysis overestimates model-specificity\"; (C) Cross-benchmark gradient confirmed → additional dimension of conditionality.\n16\t\n17\t**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n18\t\n19\t**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent and temperature-conditional. Binary correct: G=0.611 (200 items), G≥0.80 requires ~510 items (all facets random) or ~414 items (practical: precision+temperature fixed). Text-exact-match: G=0.007 (temperature dominates at 79.7%), unreliable at any temperature >0. Temperature-stratified analysis reveals binary G degrades monotonically (T=0.0: 0.760 → T=0.7: 0.549). G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116) because it accounts for prompt×item (4.6%), seed×item (4.7%), and residual (21.6%) dimensions that IRT ignores. Current practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yield G=0.611 — below the 0.80 threshold for dependable measurement.\n20\t\n21\t## Contributions (4 条，明确区分)\n22\t\n23\t1. **Model×item interaction as the hidden dominant source**: When model is treated as a crossed facet, model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest variance source, collapsing cross-model G from 0.611 to 0.073. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific — single-model G-studies provide false confidence. This is the first quantification of cross-model item-level disagreement in LLM evaluation via G-theory.\n24\t\n25\t2. **Confirmatory separability with metric qualification**: Infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies. Under text-exact-match scoring, precision×temperature reaches 4.15%, consistent with floating-point non-associativity. Separability is confirmed as metric-conditional and benchmark-conditional (knowledge benchmarks only).\n26\t\n27\t3. **IRT vs G-theory: the cost of multi-facet reliability**: The first direct comparison showing G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116 for MMLU) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. At IRT's recommended 116 items, G-theory G is only 0.529. This quantifies the \"hidden cost\" of evaluation dimensions invisible to IRT.\n28\t\n29\t4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory variance decomposition pipeline + D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Includes: (a) benchmark-specific reproducibility budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G), (b) temperature-stratified reliability guide, (c) model-as-facet analysis template. Addresses \"purely diagnostic\" criticism — provides actionable tools, not just warnings.\n30\t\n31\t## Methodological Robustness Package (6 验证分析)\n32\tAll completed on Llama 239,200 records:\n33\t- **Binary data not artifact**: Null simulation item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×); obs/Bernoulli ratio=1.0008\n34\t- **Fixed vs random insensitive**: Binary G ranges 0.611-0.664 across all facet classification choices\n35\t- **Bootstrap = jackknife**: CI width ratio 0.96-1.04, independence violation negligible\n36\t- **Temperature stratification**: Binary G degrades T=0.0 (0.760) → T=0.7 (0.549); text metric unreliable at all T>0\n37\t- **D-study formula verified**: Monotonicity assertion added; min_items_G80=414 (practical) / 510 (all random)\n38\t- **IRT comparison**: G-theory 3.6-4.4× more conservative than IRT; difference attributable to prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n39\t\n40\t## Story Spine\n41\t\n42\t**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n43\t\n44\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n45\t\n46\t**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n47\t\n48\t**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of 5,184 conditions across 4 models on MMLU (200 items), item selection accounts for 61% of variance in single-model evaluation but only 38% when model is treated as a crossed facet — with model×item interaction (26%) emerging as the second-largest source. Cross-model generalizability collapses from G=0.61 to G=0.07, revealing that item difficulty rankings are model-specific. G-theory requires 4× more items than IRT to achieve reliable measurement because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. Direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible (<0.1% under binary scoring). We release an open-source G-theory toolkit for practitioners to compute benchmark-specific reproducibility budgets.\"\n49\t\n50\t## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n51\t\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n52\t\n53\t## Narrative Strategy\n54\t- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n55\t- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n56\t- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n57\t- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n58\t- **Figure plan** (5 figures):\n59\t  1. **Stacked-bar: single-model vs cross-model variance decomposition** — item_id shrinks from 61% to 38%, model×item appears at 26%. The \"reveal\" figure.\n60\t  2. **G-theory crossed design diagram** — facets, levels, crossing structure\n61\t  3. **D-study projection curves** — G vs item count for binary (practical vs all-random) and text metric. Shows G≥0.80 threshold at ~414/510 items.\n62\t  4. **IRT vs G-theory item requirement comparison** — bar chart showing 116 (IRT) vs 510 (G-theory) items, with breakdown of where the extra items go (prompt×item, seed×item, residual)\n63\t  5. **Temperature-stratified G coefficient heatmap** — binary vs text metric × 3 temperatures, showing metric×temperature interaction\n64\t- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n65\t  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n66\t  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n67\t  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n68\t  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n69\t\n70\t## Venue Fit Risks\n71\t- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n72\t- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n73\t- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n74\t- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n75\t\n76\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n77\t1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n78\t2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n79\t3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n80\t4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n81\t5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n82\t6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n83\t7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n84\t8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n85\t9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n86\t10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n87\t11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n88\t12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n89\t13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n90\t14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n91\t15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n92\t\n93\t## Design Decisions\n94\t- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n95\t- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n96\t- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n97\t- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n98\t- **Model as crossed facet**: In exp-001 analysis (exp-004), model is treated as a crossed random facet in G-theory, not pooled or stratified. This properly attributes model×item interaction and avoids inflating item_id estimates.\n99\t- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n100\t- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n101\t\n102\t## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n103\t\n104\t### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n105\t**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n106\t**Defense strategy**: (a) Report correlation between Bernoulli variance p_i(1-p_i) and observed item variance — quantify how much item_id is \"mechanical\" vs informative; (b) Run parallel analysis on text_exact_match (continuous metric) to show variance structure is robust across metrics; (c) Discuss generalized G-theory (Brennan 2001 Ch.12) in Limitations; (d) Cite Lalor et al. (2019) who used IRT (also assumes latent continuous) on binary NLP data without this criticism being fatal.\n107\t\n108\t### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n109\t**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n110\t**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n111\t\n112\t### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n113\t**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n114\t**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n115\t\n116\t### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n117\t**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n118\t**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n119\t\n120\t### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n121\t**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n122\t**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n123\t\n124\t### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n125\t**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n126\t**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n127\t\n128\t### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n129\t**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n130\t**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n131\t\n132\t### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n133\t**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n134\t**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n135\t\n136\t### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n137\t**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n138\t**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n139\t\n140\t## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\n141\tKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n142\t- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n143\t- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n144\t- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n145\t- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n146\t- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n147\t- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n148\t- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n149\t- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical)\n150\t- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n151\t- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n152\t- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n153\t- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n154\t- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n155\t- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n156\t- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df06a013-e402-44e4-948d-a7387355c397","uuid":"a12e9eb1-de46-4536-a636-dd48282d6abc","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:39:25.374Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044739/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n\n## Title\nReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Model-Conditional Item Dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in single-model LLM evaluation (61.1% on MMLU), but model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest source when model is treated as a crossed facet — collapsing cross-model generalizability (G: 0.611→0.073). Item difficulty rankings are model-specific: two models with similar aggregate accuracy (Llama 66.4% vs Gemma 73.2%) disagree dramatically at the item level. This means single-model variance decompositions provide a false sense of reliability. Cross-benchmark gradient (pending exp-002) is hypothesized to further modulate this structure.\n**Empirical anchors (exp-001, 2 models, 325,600 records)**: Single-model item_id=61.1%, cross-model item_id=37.9% (↓23pp), model×item=26.2%, cross-model G=0.073. D-study: even 10 models yield G=0.179 — model×item interaction is irreducible by adding models.\n**Fallback narratives**: (A) If 4-model analysis confirms 2-model trend → \"model×item interaction is a fundamental barrier to cross-model generalizability\"; (B) If 4-model analysis shows stabilization (item_id rebounds) → \"2-model analysis overestimates model-specificity\"; (C) Cross-benchmark gradient confirmed → additional dimension of conditionality.\n\n**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n\n**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent and temperature-conditional. Binary correct: G=0.611 (200 items), G≥0.80 requires ~510 items (all facets random) or ~414 items (practical: precision+temperature fixed). Text-exact-match: G=0.007 (temperature dominates at 79.7%), unreliable at any temperature >0. Temperature-stratified analysis reveals binary G degrades monotonically (T=0.0: 0.760 → T=0.7: 0.549). G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116) because it accounts for prompt×item (4.6%), seed×item (4.7%), and residual (21.6%) dimensions that IRT ignores. Current practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yield G=0.611 — below the 0.80 threshold for dependable measurement.\n\n## Contributions (4 条，明确区分)\n\n1. **Model×item interaction as the hidden dominant source**: When model is treated as a crossed facet, model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest variance source, collapsing cross-model G from 0.611 to 0.073. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific — single-model G-studies provide false confidence. This is the first quantification of cross-model item-level disagreement in LLM evaluation via G-theory.\n\n2. **Confirmatory separability with metric qualification**: Infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies. Under text-exact-match scoring, precision×temperature reaches 4.15%, consistent with floating-point non-associativity. Separability is confirmed as metric-conditional and benchmark-conditional (knowledge benchmarks only).\n\n3. **IRT vs G-theory: the cost of multi-facet reliability**: The first direct comparison showing G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116 for MMLU) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. At IRT's recommended 116 items, G-theory G is only 0.529. This quantifies the \"hidden cost\" of evaluation dimensions invisible to IRT.\n\n4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory variance decomposition pipeline + D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Includes: (a) benchmark-specific reproducibility budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G), (b) temperature-stratified reliability guide, (c) model-as-facet analysis template. Addresses \"purely diagnostic\" criticism — provides actionable tools, not just warnings.\n\n## Methodological Robustness Package (6 验证分析)\nAll completed on Llama 239,200 records:\n- **Binary data not artifact**: Null simulation item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×); obs/Bernoulli ratio=1.0008\n- **Fixed vs random insensitive**: Binary G ranges 0.611-0.664 across all facet classification choices\n- **Bootstrap = jackknife**: CI width ratio 0.96-1.04, independence violation negligible\n- **Temperature stratification**: Binary G degrades T=0.0 (0.760) → T=0.7 (0.549); text metric unreliable at all T>0\n- **D-study formula verified**: Monotonicity assertion added; min_items_G80=414 (practical) / 510 (all random)\n- **IRT comparison**: G-theory 3.6-4.4× more conservative than IRT; difference attributable to prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n\n**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of 5,184 conditions across 4 models on MMLU (200 items), item selection accounts for 61% of variance in single-model evaluation but only 38% when model is treated as a crossed facet — with model×item interaction (26%) emerging as the second-largest source. Cross-model generalizability collapses from G=0.61 to G=0.07, revealing that item difficulty rankings are model-specific. G-theory requires 4× more items than IRT to achieve reliable measurement because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. Direct infrastructure×methodology interactions are negligible (<0.1% under binary scoring). We release an open-source G-theory toolkit for practitioners to compute benchmark-specific reproducibility budgets.\"\n\n## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n\n## Narrative Strategy\n- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n- **Figure plan** (5 figures):\n  1. **Stacked-bar: single-model vs cross-model variance decomposition** — item_id shrinks from 61% to 38%, model×item appears at 26%. The \"reveal\" figure.\n  2. **G-theory crossed design diagram** — facets, levels, crossing structure\n  3. **D-study projection curves** — G vs item count for binary (practical vs all-random) and text metric. Shows G≥0.80 threshold at ~414/510 items.\n  4. **IRT vs G-theory item requirement comparison** — bar chart showing 116 (IRT) vs 510 (G-theory) items, with breakdown of where the extra items go (prompt×item, seed×item, residual)\n  5. **Temperature-stratified G coefficient heatmap** — binary vs text metric × 3 temperatures, showing metric×temperature interaction\n- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n\n## Design Decisions\n- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n- **Model as crossed facet**: In exp-001 analysis (exp-004), model is treated as a crossed random facet in G-theory, not pooled or stratified. This properly attributes model×item interaction and avoids inflating item_id estimates.\n- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n\n## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n\n### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n**Defense strategy**: (a) Report correlation between Bernoulli variance p_i(1-p_i) and observed item variance — quantify how much item_id is \"mechanical\" vs informative; (b) Run parallel analysis on text_exact_match (continuous metric) to show variance structure is robust across metrics; (c) Discuss generalized G-theory (Brennan 2001 Ch.12) in Limitations; (d) Cite Lalor et al. (2019) who used IRT (also assumes latent continuous) on binary NLP data without this criticism being fatal.\n\n### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n\n### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n\n### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n\n### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n\n### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n\n### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n\n### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n\n### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n\n## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\nKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical)\n- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n","numLines":157,"startLine":1,"totalLines":157}}}
